  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1244 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge to the west is forecast to mainly remain \nstationary over the Four Corners region, keeping us in northwesterly \nflow throughout the period. Guidance indicates the ridge may begin \nto amplify on Wednesday, and begin to de-amplify come Friday as a \ntrough moves through the US/Canadian Border. Shortwaves are expected \nto move through the area during the week and into the weekend, \nincreasing precipitation chances. \n\nHigh temperatures for the period will generally remain in the 90s. \nWednesday appears to be the \"coolest\" day, with highs ranging from \nthe mid 80s to low 90s. Saturday is forecast to be the warmest, with \nhighs in the upper 90s and the potential for a few locations to \nreach the low 100s. If the ridge begins to move eastward, then \nhigher than forecasted temperatures could be seen. Winds are \nexpected to remain calm to breezy throughout the period. \n\nPrecipitation chances are present throughout the period. Wednesday \nand Thursday hold the highest potential, with PoPs in the 20-50% \nrange, as shortwaves move through the area during the afternoon and \nevening hours. While instability and shear parameters appear \nsufficient for severe storms, model guidance suggests some \nvariability in CAPE and shear values, which could lead to less \norganized storms and reduced severe chances. Precipitation chances \nthen begins to taper off to 5-25% for Friday through Sunday.",
